.page play 1 play WAV files, songs and raw audio

# SYNOPSIS
*play* [*-q*] [*-v* _PERCENT_] [*-g* _MS_] _FILE.wav_ | _FILE.tms_ ...
*play* *-r* _RATE_ *-c* _1|2_ *-b* _BITS_ [*-q*] _FILE_ ...
*play* [*-s* _SECONDS_] [*-t* _SECONDS_] [*-L*] _FILE.tms_
*play* *-i* [*-t* _SECONDS_] _FILE.tms_ ...
*play* *-w* _OUT.wav_ [*-s* _SECONDS_] [*-t* _SECONDS_] _FILE.tms_

# DESCRIPTION
`play` sends audio files to the sound card through `/dev/audio` and returns when the last one has been heard. A file that starts like a WAV file is played with the format its header gives (PCM, 8, 16, 24 or 32 bits, mono or stereo, any rate from 4000 to 192000 Hz). A file that is named `*.tms` or starts with the word `tms` is a song (see tms(5)) and is synthesized as it plays. Anything else is taken to be raw samples: little endian, with the format named by `-r`, `-c` and `-b` (44100 Hz, stereo, 16 bits if they are left out). The file name `-` reads raw samples from standard input.

The kernel mixes `play` with every other program that is making sound, converts the rate and the sample size, and applies the master volume (see `volume(1)`). Ctrl+C stops the sound at once. If the machine has no sound device `play` says so and exits with status 1, except for `-i` and `-w`, which do not need one.

# OPTIONS
@ *-q*
    Do not print the format of each file or the title of each song.
@ *-v* _PERCENT_
    The volume of this playback alone, 0 to 100, on top of the master volume.
@ *-g* _MS_
    Wait this long between files with the sound device still open, so the stream runs dry in between (a test of how the mixer handles silence).
@ *-r* _RATE_, *-c* _CHANNELS_, *-b* _BITS_
    Raw input: frames per second, 1 or 2 channels, 8 (unsigned), 16, 24 or 32 (signed) bits.
@ *-s* _SECONDS_, *-t* _SECONDS_
    For a song: start this far into it, and play (or measure, or render) only this long.
@ *-L*
    Repeat a song for ever instead of playing it once.
@ *-i*
    Describe a song instead of playing it: title, tempo, channels and their instruments, length, anything the parser had to fix, and, from rendering the first 30 seconds (or `-t`), its peak level, loudness and how much of it is silence.
@ *-w* _OUT.wav_
    Render a song to a 44100 Hz stereo WAV file instead of playing it.

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if there is no sound device or a file could not be played; 2 for a usage error.

# EXAMPLES
    play ~/Music/shrine.wav
    play -q -v 40 a.wav b.wav
    play /ScarletDevilMansion/share/music/moonlit-lake.tms
    play -i -t 10 mysong.tms
    play -w /tmp/mysong.wav mysong.tms
    play -r 22050 -c 1 -b 8 voice.raw

# SEE ALSO
beep(1), volume(1), jukebox(1), tms(5), audio(7)
